WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:02.320
- And... rolling.

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:03.930
I can't clickbait this video.

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:07.380
I mean, I could, but it wouldn't
really be fair to anyone involved.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.790
I know the title and the thumbnail
aren't going to pull loads of people in,

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.050
but I hope that you can trust me

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:14.070
when I say: I promise this
story is really interesting.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:15.110
Because it is.

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:18.230
This is one of the best things I've found
while researching in years.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.620
So this starts when I'm
looking up old television shows

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.800
and I'm about three
research tangents deep,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.950
and I find this incredible line
in a newspaper article from 1997:

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.490
"The microwave was invented to meet a need

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.713
"to heat hamsters humanely
in 1950s laboratories."

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.663
And I read that and I thought:
that's ridiculous. No, it wasn't.

00:00:38.165 --> 00:00:38.998
Was it?

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:43.440
There's a well-known story
about the engineer

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.010
who "invented the microwave".

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:47.020
Percy Spencer, just after World War II,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.960
was working on one of the
new military-grade radars

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.990
while it was active,
and he realised

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:53.700
that the peanut bar in his
back pocket had melted.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.390
Which implies that the radar also
nearly cooked part of him as well?

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.210
Unshielded radars are really dangerous,
but it was the 1940s.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:02.650
The attitude to safety was a bit more lax.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:06.960
Now it wasn't a surprise that
electromagnetic radiation could heat stuff.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.120
That had been known for a while.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.750
People had actually been using it
for medical therapies for a while.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.020
Different wavelengths
had different effects,

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.570
but Percy Spencer was the
first person to realise

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:18.320
you could cook efficiently
with the wavelengths

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.800
that were put out by
radar, and then realised

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.880
that you could sell that,
and then realised,

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:26.680
well, I could turn that
into a usable product.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:27.920
Because a massive radar dish

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.750
is basically the same
technology as this microwave.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.220
I'm not going to try and
take this thing apart.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.140
I'm not qualified.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.130
And there is a high-voltage
capacitor in here

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:36.960
which can carry enough charge to kill.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.370
Don't mess about with these
unless you know what you're doing.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.823
But inside here is...

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.910
Inside here in the bit you can't access,

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:49.500
there's a microwave generator
called a magnetron.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.400
It moves electric current past
a big magnet with a hole in it.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.160
And because... physics, that
produces electromagnetic radiation.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:57.890
Tune the size of the hole in the magnet,

00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.690
you get different
frequencies of radiation.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:03.103
The best analogy I've found is that
it's like blowing air over glass bottles

00:02:03.103 --> 00:02:05.610
and changing the pitch by
changing the amount of water inside.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.144
So...
[glass whistling at different tones].

00:02:09.144 --> 00:02:10.570
That, but with electricity.

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:12.280
...look, I don't have a physics degree.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.470
Long story short, a magnetron
converts electricity

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:17.520
into electromagnetic radiation.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.810
You send pulses of that
radiation out into the sky

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.120
and you listen for the
echoes, you've got radar

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:24.130
and you've helped win World War II.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.500
Or you could put a magnetron

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.440
inside a safe radiation-proof box
called a Faraday cage,

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:29.450
you tune the wavelengths

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:31.670
so that water absorbs
the radiation easily,

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:35.010
and you've got a microwave.
And you can cook lunch.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.020
The technical term is
"diathermic heating".

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.270
In less than a year after the
melting peanut bar incident,

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:44.200
which could so easily have been, like,
the "cooked hand incident" or worse,

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:45.810
but less than a year after that

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.760
that engineer's company,
defence contractor Raytheon,

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.600
they release the first
commercial microwave.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.110
It was called the Radarange.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.120
It was the size of a fridge.
It had to be actively cooled.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.390
It was incredibly expensive

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.240
and it was designed for
big commercial kitchens.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.290
That was 1947.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:03.780
The first microwave as we'd know it today,

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.980
a cheap box that you plug in,
that was from the late 1960s

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.730
once magnetron technology
had moved on a bit.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:12.960
At no point in that
well-referenced, well-known story

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.837
does anyone mention heating hamsters
in laboratories in the 1950s.

00:03:17.837 --> 00:03:19.370
[microwave dings]

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:21.510
So that should be pretty
easy to disprove, right?

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:22.600
It's going to be an urban legend.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.440
There are a lot of
urban legends like that.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.010
So I started doing some journal searches

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.650
and pretty quickly I found
papers from the 1950s

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:32.290
talking about the reanimation
of frozen rats and hamsters.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.730
Those papers were written by scientists

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:35.930
at the National Institute
for Medical Research,

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:37.400
a government laboratory in Mill Hill,

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.240
in suburban North London,
about half a mile that way.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:41.690
I would have filmed closer to that,

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:43.610
but unfortunately, it was
pulled down a few years ago

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:46.070
and the whole area is now a
load of new-build housing.

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:48.910
Anyway, National Institute
For Medical Research.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:52.270
In the 1950s, that research
included cryobiology,

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:54.570
the effects of extreme
cold on bodies and tissue.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:59.630
Scientists at that lab froze rats and
hamsters beyond the point of death.

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:01.840
They used ice and a bath
of propylene glycol,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.820
which is airplane de-icer,
held at below zero,

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:07.410
and at that point, the animal is dead
and will not recover on its own.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:10.500
No heart rate, no breathing,
core temperature around 1°C,

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:14.740
somewhere between 10 and 50%
of the animal's body water frozen to ice.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:17.250
And then by applying hot spatulas
to the chest

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.640
or high-intensity hot beams of light

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.870
the researchers would try
to warm the animals up

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:24.650
and bring them back to life.
The success rate wasn't great

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.010
and the animals were
often left with burns,

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.820
but it did sometimes work.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:30.360
We'll get to why they
were doing that later!

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.400
But by 1955, one of the researchers
has the idea to use a magnetron,

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:36.324
reasoning that it'd be a lot more safe
and humane

00:04:36.324 --> 00:04:39.070
to reheat the whole animal at once
rather than risk burning it.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:40.730
Actually, I say one of the researchers,

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:43.560
there's a name on that paper
that might be a bit familiar.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.370
James Lovelock, one of the
last independent scientists,

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:50.049
born in 1919, back when "scientist"
could be an entire job description

00:04:50.049 --> 00:04:52.730
rather than "botanist" or "physicist"
or "computational linguist".

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.330
Someone who worked across disciplines

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:57.820
on whatever they found interesting
or whatever they were hired to do.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:59.920
Lovelock is best known
for inventing Gaia theory,

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.540
the idea of earth as a
self-regulating system.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.700
He also invented the
electronic capture detector

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:08.180
which found CFCs in the atmosphere
and helped discover the ozone hole.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:11.110
Hardware that he helped design
for NASA is sitting on Mars

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:14.470
because it landed there with
the Viking probes in 1976.

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:17.670
And one of the early projects
in his career, back in the 1950s

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:21.736
was at the National Institute for Medical Research
freezing rats and hamsters,

00:05:21.736 --> 00:05:24.483
and he had the idea to use
microwave di-a-ther-my...,

00:05:24.483 --> 00:05:26.241
dia-tha-my? dia-tha...?

00:05:26.607 --> 00:05:27.815
[keyboard clicks]

00:05:29.596 --> 00:05:30.429
- "Diathermy."

00:05:30.429 --> 00:05:31.550
- To reheat them.

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:33.850
But the papers and
anecdotes from back then

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:35.090
all seem to contradict each other.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:36.540
The timeline is difficult
to piece together.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:40.630
There are reliable sources with
completely different stories and claims.

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:43.060
So I went to ask him about it.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:45.020
- "Do you know you're
the first person to come

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.140
"and ask me about the freezing work?"

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:49.410
- Because James Lovelock, at 101 years old

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.020
lives somewhere vaguely round here
on the south coast of England,

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:54.000
goes for a walk along the coast every day,

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.690
and was happy to be interviewed.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:57.890
- One biologist, Audrey Smith,

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:02.050
was able to revive a hamster
that had been frozen.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:06.060
When they woke up, they got a
gigantic burn all across their chest

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:09.050
and that must have been pretty
painful, and it was messy.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.510
I thought, this is a lousy way to do it.

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:13.837
So I said, "Why don't you use diathermy?"

00:06:13.837 --> 00:06:22.220
For 10 shillings of my own money
I bought a surplus RAF transmitter,

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:27.292
joined it up, and sure enough,
it poured out 30 watts at 30 megahertz, 

00:06:27.292 --> 00:06:32.050
Which is just about right.
And I took it into the lab the next day.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.887
And I said, "Look, here's your diathermy."

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:38.790
I thought this is very untidy,
made some inquiries

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:42.040
and sure enough, there were
10-centimetre magnetrons

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:47.370
that had been invented by Boot and Randall
at Birmingham University during the war.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:50.370
To get hold of one of those is not easy.

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:53.660
And this is where being in a
government department helped a bit.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:55.127
So I just made a few inquiries.

00:06:55.127 --> 00:06:56.120
"How do I get hold...?"

00:06:56.120 --> 00:07:00.950
And they said, "Oh, well, they're working on
them down at... just above Portsmouth."

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:05.730
I said, "Any chance of borrowing
one of your magnetrons?"

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:07.060
And he said, "Oh no."

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:12.340
He said, "We can give you one if you want.
It's an interesting cause, why not?"

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:18.890
I've joined this up with a
waveguide, into a metal box.

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:25.300
It wasn't so much a box. It was
just a thing made out of

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.960
chicken wire, except it's
finer than chicken wire,

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:30.760
a bit closer to mesh.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:34.600
And that was just like the
box of a microwave oven.

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:37.160
And I fed the microwaves into it.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:40.220
And I told Audrey, now,
put your hamster in there!

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:41.340
- That's a microwave.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.930
James Lovelock may not have
invented THE first microwave

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.600
but he certainly invented A microwave.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:47.830
The idea of using magnetrons
to heat stuff wasn't his,

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:51.270
but no one else was putting one
in a Faraday cage box on a desktop.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:54.710
Big commercial microwaves for restaurant
kitchens aren't the same thing, and besides,

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:57.320
they weren't even on sale in
the UK until a few years later.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:03.280
- I put a potato in it and baked it.
And it was perfectly all right.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:07.660
- The safety procedures were occasionally
a bit lax by modern standards.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:10.690
- In the course of the experiments
while we were building it

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:14.690
the thing was running open
and the radiation

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:16.660
was bouncing all round the room

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:20.892
and the light bulbs would
light up without warning.

00:08:20.892 --> 00:08:27.747
The filament just had the same wavelength
as the radiation,

00:08:27.747 --> 00:08:31.003
and it would absorb it
and light up.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:34.960
And pound notes were the funniest one.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:39.590
They'd catch fire because the
metal strip inside was

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:42.130
just about the wavelength of the magnetron.

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:45.380
- And here's the really
amazing thing, it worked.

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:49.280
- We put the hamster
in there, frozen solid.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:50.790
And I mean frozen solid.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:53.923
It was like a bit of wood
when you dropped it on the bench.

00:08:55.435 --> 00:08:58.440
Turned the thing up to full power
on the microwave,

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:02.460
which was about a
kilowatt coming out of it.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:05.700
And with a timer.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:10.370
And after so many seconds,
the hamster woke up,

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:12.540
started wandering around.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.450
- Do I need to say,
don't try this at home?

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:16.230
I mean there are enough
people of all ages watching

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:18.050
that statistically yes,
I do need to say it.

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:20.060
Don't try this at home.
It's unnecessary cruelty.

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:22.910
You won't be doing medical research,
you'll be doing a crime.

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:24.370
Context is really important,

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:28.030
but also the research has been done!
More than 50 years ago.

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:30.660
There are charts of
survival rate and heating,

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:32.520
and it turns out that with a microwave,

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:35.770
because that's what this was,
with a precise calibrated microwave

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:39.510
and with artificial respiration
to start pumping oxygen around the body

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:43.230
almost every rodent they froze
was successfully reanimated.

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:44.920
That's the word that the biologists use.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:47.400
Reanimation. Healthy, behaving as usual

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:50.660
and given a retirement at an animal home,
according to one of the papers.

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:55.520
Back from being frozen to death,
sometimes multiple times.

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:58.420
So the last question is: why?

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:03.810
- Well, this could be of immense
importance to medicine.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:11.610
So for preserving tissues for transfer
and also for blood transfusions

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:15.730
because at the moment blood
was kept at the blood bank

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:18.570
for three weeks and then
thrown down the sink.

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:21.480
- Imagine if that had worked
on bigger animals.

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:23.820
Back then humanity did not
know what the limits were.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.320
This was an open question.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:28.580
The reason that all those 1960s science fiction writers
and comic book authors

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:34.310
had people being frozen in time or
astronauts being sent off in cryosleep

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:36.510
might be because, for a little while,

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:38.800
it looked like that might
actually be possible.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:42.100
If freezing and diathermic reheating
worked on hamsters...

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:43.370
then maybe it would work on rabbits.

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:45.490
If it worked on rabbits, then
maybe it'd work on primates.

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:49.290
And if it worked on primates...
then maybe it'd work on humans.

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:51.460
It didn't, unfortunately.
It doesn't scale up.

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:54.148
- You cannot freeze a human.

00:10:54.148 --> 00:11:02.770
It's partly a matter of how quickly you
can get the anti-freeze agent

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:04.960
to diffuse into the cells.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:06.820
A human's too big.

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:12.100
It's just a matter of size.
A hamster is an acceptable size.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:14.160
- Sometimes research hits a brick wall.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:16.740
Find out a neat thing
about hamsters, check.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:20.033
Casually invent a microwave and
then don't commercialise it. Check.

00:11:20.033 --> 00:11:23.860
Find a way to safely send
astronauts to other planets?

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:26.330
Not this time, unfortunately,
but the researchers tried

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:28.920
and that feels like a story
that shouldn't be buried

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:31.850
as a vague anecdote in a
newspaper from years ago.

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:34.710
- I think in an awful lot of science

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:39.690
where there's a success
it's not a scientific thing.

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:43.450
It's an invention, which
is something quite different.

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:48.050
And I think personally I'm much more
of an inventor than a scientist.

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:49.740
- My thanks to James Lovelock for his time.

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:53.000
His latest book "Novacene" is part memoir
and part look to the future

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.933
and there's a link to
it in the description.

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:57.580
- Oh, that was a mistake.

00:11:57.580 --> 00:11:58.413
That was... I...

00:12:00.998 --> 00:12:03.000
Oh that's going to be a nightmare to wash.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.280
All right, well, I guess I don't
get a second take, 'cos...

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:06.903
I put that on low!

